{{Japanese name|Takanaka}}

{{nihongo|'''Fumihiro Takanaka'''|高中 文博|Takanaka Fumihiro}} (12 June 1884 – 8 April 1959) was a Sanese diplomat and politician who served as the 28th [[Prime Minister of Okaiken]] from December 1954 to January 1956. A career diplomat, he served as chief Sanese delegate in the armistice negotiations that ended the [[Great Adonian War]] in the East Fosian Theater, and as Minister of Foreign Affairs in the transition government of Prince [[Tago Shoka|Tago Shōka]] and the reconstruction government of [[Munenori Mitsui]]. As Prime Minister he established the [[Okaiken Self-Defense Forces]] under strictly civilian control in December 1954, dissolved the Imperial General Staff, and oversaw the political realignment of 1955.<ref name="bitter">Takanaka Fumihiro, ''The Bitter Peace'' (Okami: Okami University Press, 1961; written 1958, published posthumously).</ref>

== Early life ==
Takanaka was born in [[Miyagi Prefecture]] in 1884, the son of a provincial schoolteacher. His modest origins set him apart from the aristocratic and military elites that dominated the wartime establishment. He studied political science at the [[Okami University|Oka-kyo Imperial University]] in [[Okami]], where he developed a particular interest in international law; among his contemporaries at the university, three years his junior, was [[Kōki Hirota]], the future wartime Prime Minister, though the two were not close. Graduating in 1907, Takanaka entered the Ministry of Foreign Affairs the same year.

== Diplomatic career ==
Takanaka served in diplomatic postings across the Shendan region. His fluency in the Zong language and his understanding of Fosian geopolitics made him a valuable asset in the complex diplomatic environment of the interwar period. He served as a junior diplomat during the negotiation of several trade agreements with [[Zong]] and was present at the 1935 Shendan Maritime Conference as an observer. By the 1940s he was regarded as one of the ministry's foremost specialists on the Shendan basin.

His postings included service at the consulate in a major Zong port city and three years at the embassy in Moselle, where he formed the view that the Empire's alliance with Zong was a strategic liability rather than an asset — that it bound Okaiken to a partner whose ambitions in the Shendan would eventually collide with the Illypnian powers. The assessment was unwelcome within the ministry, where the alliance was treated as the foundation of foreign policy, and it slowed his advancement through the ranks. Colleagues nevertheless came to consult him before every major negotiation touching the basin, and his memoranda on the balance of Shendan naval power circulated well beyond the ministry during the rearmament debates of the 1930s.

== Deputy Foreign Minister (1947–1951) ==
The escalation of the [[Great Adonian War]] brought Takanaka back to Okami. In 1947, as the Empire entered the second phase of the East Fosian Theater, he was appointed Deputy Foreign Minister under Prime Minister [[Kōki Hirota]], with responsibility for the alliance with Zong and for the increasingly fraught relationship with [[Moselle]] — a member of the Illypnian coalition that nonetheless remained Okaiken's most important channel to the Illypnian powers. He continued in the post under Hirota's successor, Kiichirō Hiranuma. Mosellan diplomats regarded Takanaka as a reasonable interlocutor who understood the necessity of a negotiated settlement — a perception that would prove consequential when the time came to seek one.

== Foreign Minister and chief armistice negotiator (1951–1953) ==
Prince Tago Shōka retained Takanaka at the Foreign Ministry when he formed his transition government in October 1951, and named him chief Sanese delegate in the armistice negotiations. The talks built on the back-channel contacts that had run through Mosellan intermediaries and the Sansin embassy in Okami since the final months of the Hiranuma government; the Mosellan Peace Initiative of the spring of 1952 had failed when the Illypnian coalition demanded unconditional withdrawal from all occupied territories as the price of any settlement. In late 1951 the transition government authorized formal contacts with the Volisanian diplomatic corps, which had maintained neutrality throughout the theater and held the confidence of both sides, and the negotiations that produced the armistice followed.<ref name="volisania">Ministry of Foreign Affairs of Okaiken, ''The Volisanian Mediation: Negotiating Records, 1951–1952'' (Okami: Ministry of Foreign Affairs, 1956).</ref>

The terms Takanaka's delegation negotiated were harsh but not Carthaginian. Okaiken retained its sovereignty and territorial integrity — there would be no occupation, no dissolution of the government — but renounced all overseas possessions, agreed to complete withdrawal from [[Nakamizu]] and from [[Kwangju]] and the Shendan territories within the armistice's deadlines, and accepted the international administration of the [[Kazan Islands]]. Prince Tago signed the [[Armistice of Okami]] for the Empire at the Imperial Palace on 28 April 1952; the delegation Takanaka led had negotiated every clause.

[[File:Armistice of Okami Signing 1952.png|thumb|right|200px|The signing of the Armistice of Okami on 28 April 1952; Prince Tago signed for the Empire, at the head of the delegation Takanaka led]]

That evening Takanaka broadcast the armistice terms to the nation by radio: "the nation has survived; the nation will endure. The peace is bitter, but it is peace."<ref name="bitter" />

[[File:Takanaka Radio Address 1952.png|thumb|left|200px|Takanaka's radio address to the nation on the armistice terms, 28 April 1952]]

As Foreign Minister, Takanaka signed the [[Okaiken-Volisania Mutual Security Assistance Pact]] on the day of the armistice, establishing Volisania as Okaiken's primary security partner in the post-war order. His ministry carried out the armistice's diplomatic execution: the orderly withdrawal from Kwangju, completed by the end of 1952, the transfer of the Kazan Islands to international administration, and the limited Echian military advisory arrangement that supported the demobilization period. The advisory arrangement concluded on his ministry's initiative in 1952 was later expanded into the full security cooperation agreement signed by the Mitsui government.

== Foreign Minister under Mitsui (1953–1954) ==
Takanaka remained at the Foreign Ministry when Mitsui formed the first party government of the post-war era in October 1953. He negotiated the Sansian-Sanese Basic Relations Treaty of 1954, which exchanged ambassadors with [[Sansi]] for the first time since the war and left the territorial and reparations questions for later settlement, and he led the diplomatic work of Okaiken's completed accession to the [[Adonian Community]]. He entered politics in 1953, joining the [[Progressive Party of Okaiken|Progressive Party]] and winning a seat in the [[House of Commoners of Okaiken|House of Commoners]] at the general election of that year.

== Premiership (1954–1956) ==
=== Formation ===
The Self-Defense Forces Act, drafted in 1954 to rebuild the nation's armed forces under strictly civilian control, broke Mitsui's coalition when the Socialist Party withdrew its support. Mitsui resigned on 7 December 1954, and the Progressive Party turned to Takanaka — the negotiator of the peace — to carry the rearmament legislation through a fragmented Diet. Appointed Prime Minister on 7 December 1954 at the head of a minority Progressive government, Takanaka secured the Act's passage within the month with the support of conservative members.

=== Establishment of the Okaiken Self-Defense Forces ===
The [[Okaiken Self-Defense Forces]] were proclaimed in December 1954, replacing the remnants of the Imperial military. The Act placed the new forces under a civilian Defence Agency and prohibited their operation outside Okaiken's territory; the Imperial General Staff, the institution that had carried the nation into the war, was formally dissolved and its planning functions transferred to civilian ministries.<ref name="osdf">Defence Agency of Okaiken, ''The Founding of the Okaiken Self-Defense Forces: An Official History'' (Okami, 1959).</ref> Takanaka told the Diet that the rebuilt forces would be "a shield, and nothing else." The establishment completed the transition the armistice had begun: the armed forces of the old order were replaced by forces subordinate to the civilian government.

=== Domestic policy ===
Takanaka's domestic agenda was dominated by the completion of the transition begun under Tago and Mitsui: the final demobilization of the Imperial Army and Navy, the completion of the release of political prisoners held under the wartime public-order statutes, the formal abolition of the Prevention Custody system, and the winding down of the Controlled Economy ordinances. The reconstruction program continued to expand under the Emergency Reconstruction Act framework; unemployment remained high as military industry converted to civilian production, and food rationing, progressively relaxed since 1953, would not be fully abolished until 1956.

=== Foreign policy ===
Takanaka's government concluded the Zong-Sanese Friendship Treaty of 1955, which replaced the dissolved military relationship of the [[Mitoyagi-Zong Treaty]] with a framework for commercial and diplomatic cooperation, preserving the essential diplomatic relationship while eliminating the provisions that had drawn Okaiken into the war. Okaiken's first full years of membership in the [[Adonian Community]] fell to his government, which seated the nation's first delegations to the Community's institutions. The Volisania security pact and the Echia cooperation agreement, consolidated in these years, formed the twin pillars of Sanese security policy for the decade that followed.

=== The realignment of 1955 and resignation ===
The political realignment of 1955 — the merger of the main conservative parties and the reunion of the socialist groups — transformed the party system that Takanaka headed. Historians credit his management of the Diet through the transition with sparing the country the constitutional crisis the merger might have produced.<ref name="realign">Arai Toshiko, ''The Realignment of 1955'' (Okami: Okami University Press, 1974).</ref> When the Progressive Party voted in January 1956 to join the new conservative alignment, Takanaka declined to follow it. He resigned the premiership on 11 January 1956 and was succeeded by [[Ichirō Hatoyama]].

== Later life and death ==
Takanaka retired from public life on leaving office. He completed his manuscript memoirs in 1958 but published nothing in his lifetime; the memoirs appeared posthumously in 1961 under the title ''The Bitter Peace'' and remain an essential source for the armistice negotiations.<ref name="bitter" /> He died in [[Okami]] on 8 April 1959 at the age of 74.

== Legacy ==
Takanaka is remembered as the diplomat who negotiated the armistice and as the prime minister who placed the rebuilt armed forces under civilian control. The distinction between a defeated nation and a nation that had fought to a standstill — the distinction his delegation negotiated into the armistice's text — became the foundation of Okaiken's post-war identity. Critics of the settlement, then and since, have argued that the surrender of the overseas territories and the acceptance of Adonian Community membership as a condition of peace conceded too much; the prevailing historical judgment is that the terms reflected the strategic reality of 1952.

